,title,link,text,language,family
0,Jino language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jino_language,"The exact classification of Jino within the Loloish branch of Sino-Tibetan language family remains uncertain. Jino is classified as a Southern Loloish (Hanoish) language by Ziwo Lama (2012), [2] but as a Central Loloish language by Bradley (2007). [11] Jino is also classified as a Southern Loloish language in Satterthwaite-Phillips' (2011) computational phylogenetic analysis of the Lolo ...","Jinuo, Youle",Sino-Tibetan
1,#Jinuo perform traditional song and dance - YouTube,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xac_4oZYRL4,"A fragment of a #folk #music show from #jinuo/ #jino, one of 56 #chinese #ethnicminority, village.Enjoy #traditional song from #xishuangbanna prefecture in #...","Jinuo, Youle",Sino-Tibetan
2,"JiNuo Drum Dance, TeMaoKe Festival, Yunnan China - YouTube",https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=j0VEYvI5GRg,"The local government and Jinuo cultural inheritors have promoted the living inheritance of this ancient art by setting up training centers, organizing festival performances, and teaching on campus.","Jinuo, Youle",Sino-Tibetan
3,Jinuo Bamboo Tube Music (Qike Bugu)_Baiduwiki,https://baike.baidu.com/en/item/Jinuo+Bamboo+Tube+Music+(Qike+Bugu)/122959,"Jinuo Bamboo Tube Music (Qike Bugu) is a traditional music form of the Jinuo people in Yunnan Province, characterized primarily by the performance using bamboo instruments. ""Qike Bugu"" refers to the instrument combination and playing techniques. It was included in the Yunnan Province Fifth Batch of Provincial Intangible Cultural Heritage Representative Project List on December 27, 2022.","Jinuo, Youle",Sino-Tibetan
4,Jinuo People Preserve Bamboo Music Traditions in Yunnan's Mountains,https://khabarasia.com/nature/20240208-jinuo-people-preserve-bamboo-music-traditions-in-yunnans-mountains/,"Discover how the Jinuo ethnic group in Yunnan's Xishuangbanna preserves their unique bamboo music traditions, reflecting a deep connection with nature and cultural heritage.","Jinuo, Youle",Sino-Tibetan
5,"Jinuo, Youle Language (JIU) - L1 & L2 Speakers, Status ... - Ethnologue",https://www.ethnologue.com/language/jiu/,"Youle Jinuo is an endangered indigenous language of China. It belongs to the Sino-Tibetan language family. The language is used as a first language by all adults in the ethnic community, but not all young people. It is not known to be taught in schools.","Jinuo, Youle",Sino-Tibetan
6,Jinuo Tribe's Bamboo Melodies: A Harmony with Nature in Yunnan,https://myglobalnews.net/nature/20240208-jinuo-tribes-bamboo-melodies-a-harmony-with-nature-in-yunnan/,"Discover the Jinuo tribe's harmonious relationship with bamboo in Yunnan Province, where traditional music and sustainable living blend seamlessly with nature.","Jinuo, Youle",Sino-Tibetan
7,"Jinuo Village, Jinghong - An Immersive Experience of Jinuo Culture",https://chinatripedia.com/jinuo-village-jinghong-an-immersive-experience-of-jinuo-culture/,"In 2014, based on a deep understanding of Jinuo ethnic music, dance, and cultural heritage, the live performance ""Youle Youle"" was created to promote cultural experiences. The performance showcases various aspects of Jinuo culture, from agriculture, hunting, and brewing to clothing, customs, and goddess worship.","Jinuo, Youle",Sino-Tibetan
8,The Jinuo people: China's last-recognized ethnic minority known for ...,http://english.scio.gov.cn/videos/2024-06/13/content_117251203.htm,"The Jinuo ethnic group is the last ethnic minority officially recognized in China. According to the ethnic group's folklore, their ancestors emerged from a giant drum, which is why the Jinuo people have revered the big drum for generations, considering it a sacred object.","Jinuo, Youle",Sino-Tibetan
9,Music culture in Southwest China promotes ethnic tourism,https://www.globaltimes.cn/content/1154119.shtml,"Jinuo drums in Jinuo Village in Southwest China's Yunnan Province Photo: IC In front of a giant wooden drum, Che Jie sprays wine and rice amid a group of mysterious dancers donning red, white and ...","Jinuo, Youle",Sino-Tibetan
